  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "class_subclasses",
    "file": "mruby/mrbgems/mruby-class-ext/src/class.c",
    "description": "Use-after-free caused by `class_subclasses` calling `mrb_objspace_each_objects` with a callback (`add_subclasses`) that pushes matching class objects into an array via `mrb_ary_push` without protecting the iterated object from GC. During enumeration, `mrb_ary_push`/hashing can invoke Ruby code and trigger full GC (`mrb_full_gc` inside `mrb_objspace_each_objects`), freeing a string object that is then later hashed (`mrb_str_hash` -> `mrb_byte_hash_step`) from stale memory. Root issue is unsafe object-space iteration/reentrancy in `class_subclasses` (not in `mrb_byte_hash_step`, which is just the crash site).",
    "conditions": "PoV executes Ruby that reaches `Module/Class#subclasses` (mruby-class-ext). `class_subclasses` iterates all objects and in callback appends subclass objects to an array. While iterating, operations performed during push/hash can run Ruby methods and trigger GC. Because iteration does not pin/protect transiently accessed objects against collection during this reentrant GC, an object (string used later as hash key/hash receiver) is freed and subsequently read during hash computation, producing heap-use-after-free."
  },
